#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caff9428-bce5-5aca-8e6d-3801a43b486e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Y-Increment Given All Intermediate Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c29290c2-cef4-5dd9-ba64-579a673fc214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c29290c2-cef4-5dd9-ba64-579a673fc214" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333394} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c93452e-d2d6-5e13-9bbb-3de17c87c1ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333393} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f5a7344-aeb5-56d9-b316-c49caec57822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5a7344-aeb5-56d9-b316-c49caec57822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333396} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a230cae-a71a-5aba-9dbe-0a57109e4599" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38a50945-231c-563e-aeb6-1ed87af64dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-330783" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) Method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | {{field-1}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nAll four sample slopes are already computed. So, we just need to find the increment on $y.$\n\nWe compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{29}{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot \\frac{105}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | $\\frac{35}{32}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{35}{32} \\\\  & =\\frac{67}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{29}{8}$ | $\\frac{35}{32}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{67}{32}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{67}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9740440-bdfb-588e-8f23-2eec9427e449" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Runge-Kutta Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2281f205-bf76-5103-85cf-4455fe39df17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }x+Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333399} {:answer/id #uuid "161446f8-3c75-5b10-8bdd-f7231a0786a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333400} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c1f013-b704-5050-984a-9c9fc5c74573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }x+{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333401} {:answer/id #uuid "5fe4f970-e891-50d7-8396-d0ab66c53315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fe4f970-e891-50d7-8396-d0ab66c53315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333402} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a5ffac5-bc6a-5d96-b900-5d2c46343726" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f3d1c5a-fb11-5ae6-a7be-586e29ced311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y+{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333404} {:answer/id #uuid "0b954169-4198-5baf-8e9f-173c9a6b0bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y+{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333405} {:answer/id #uuid "81da675f-c2ac-592e-876f-7105892c7666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333406} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c6e8a6-a72d-5d09-a9ce-fa1de7c4d44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9c6e8a6-a72d-5d09-a9ce-fa1de7c4d44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333407} :answer-field/id #uuid "930bdfbe-8343-56cd-a14b-b698522a0242" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333403} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492a5f4d-5228-58a0-9d02-6f21f43a73c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333409} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4c26e4-7769-59d1-9f20-06b50ef77819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333410} {:answer/id #uuid "4eacfb73-49a3-58cb-aee1-1e46f82450ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333411} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc8364f-23aa-5d91-ba23-723dec63bea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{ }{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "492a5f4d-5228-58a0-9d02-6f21f43a73c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333409} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e47189-3f06-5447-a8f6-6599aea1d6b5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a86ccb7-b48d-5d3c-a367-87e75ba45fad" :question/math-academy-id "q-331162" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nand we take a step of size $Δx$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Fill in the blanks to complete the weighted-average formula for the increment and the stage definitions, starting at the point $(x,y).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & =f({{field-1}},\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & =f(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,{{field-2}}) \\\\ {y}_{3}^{′} & =f(x+Δx,\\,{{field-3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b7117f-133f-50df-918d-4ff129a660be" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using RK4"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a955841-cccc-5acc-ad6f-4b57a08a7817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333415} {:answer/id #uuid "7578fc10-df38-5805-b3a0-02f00e9e6693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333416} {:answer/id #uuid "e41611fd-0a99-505e-9bf8-fab74df9c1af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333417} {:answer/id #uuid "eea541d4-d839-5eef-b23f-b3f3d37d6b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333418} {:answer/id #uuid "db5eea1f-648a-531e-8e05-dc6019ce884c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7578fc10-df38-5805-b3a0-02f00e9e6693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333416} :answer-field/id #uuid "5109c124-757d-5ca6-80f0-bb905a690dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26efb47a-b036-5035-b2b5-b1eebdcc61ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-331213" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y+3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $1$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-y+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =2{x}_{1}-{y}_{1}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{5}{3}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =2{x}_{2}-{y}_{2}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,\\frac{17}{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =2{x}_{3}-{y}_{3}+3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-\\frac{17}{6}+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{8}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\frac{16}{6}+\\frac{28}{6}+\\frac{30}{6}+\\frac{13}{6})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{87}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{29}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{8}{3}\\,$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{6}$ | $\\frac{29}{12}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\,\\frac{11}{4}\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(1)≈\\frac{11}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "43ab4b4b-4462-50e5-ad14-fb31a502be7d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Y-Increment Given Some Intermediate Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab66372-7bb9-52e5-9f44-dcc4bae47563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333422} {:answer/id #uuid "45086742-5628-5d78-bbee-69448dc92c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333423} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ddf5cd-4243-5251-81a9-e7ee3af2e489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45086742-5628-5d78-bbee-69448dc92c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333423} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bab03fc-704d-5f8b-b42a-af8e68529ed1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333421} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0cc5341-2efd-57d9-862c-47af01e0b04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333426} {:answer/id #uuid "099702a3-7903-5c37-8ea3-af9d6b68e8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333427} {:answer/id #uuid "ff14ab38-a8a4-5497-8835-041b8557ce92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff14ab38-a8a4-5497-8835-041b8557ce92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333428} :answer-field/id #uuid "51d586b7-4a1f-5814-918c-9f64e683232c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2935f5-9c72-5bf5-b7ae-fd72868d7400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333430} {:answer/id #uuid "8a34410a-aaa3-5bfe-9b65-ce0f5fbbdacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333431} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e1dd57-2809-5b65-89a6-930913e9e2e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2935f5-9c72-5bf5-b7ae-fd72868d7400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333430} :answer-field/id #uuid "99893eb4-447d-5565-a588-acb925776858" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333429} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fedbcdf9-10c0-573d-a924-fb3983fbf9f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333434} {:answer/id #uuid "51f081ad-0bad-5383-98ce-fbef2c8e8f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333435} {:answer/id #uuid "10976a47-b5d0-547b-b67f-343152c6e42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10976a47-b5d0-547b-b67f-343152c6e42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333436} :answer-field/id #uuid "a348944c-e01e-5a86-97c1-010ced1436c7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333433} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fbb5c3c9-47ad-5f15-8387-5db6139f190f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333438} {:answer/id #uuid "0f32ed3c-0c1e-55fc-a521-f4706377d978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333439} {:answer/id #uuid "7e861d1e-6cf7-5f2b-9218-eab25304f881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e861d1e-6cf7-5f2b-9218-eab25304f881" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333440} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c1abeb5-4d19-559f-9655-152a7a9a2a80" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333437} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebb1298d-f182-5414-ac48-9efce104b7a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333442} {:answer/id #uuid "50f4b4fd-d1c1-5ef1-92d6-56101eb60da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333443} {:answer/id #uuid "8e879357-9141-5b7f-b3b7-bc799f15e550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50f4b4fd-d1c1-5ef1-92d6-56101eb60da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333443} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4cf91a4-6ba4-5edd-a4f2-f4ddded09a6b" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed212000-5065-5f97-bba2-9e07a46089f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-339819" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-8y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method. Complete the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | {{field-6}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-8y \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & =4{x}_{1}-8{y}_{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4},\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{9}{8},\\frac{17}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & =4{x}_{2}-8{y}_{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{8}-8\\cdot \\frac{17}{16} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{1}{4},\\,1+(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{4},0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & =4{x}_{3}-8{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}-8\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-4+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot (-4)+5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}(-6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-4$ | $5$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{5}{4})≈\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2b7117f-133f-50df-918d-4ff129a660be" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using RK4"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800b9b74-39d4-5369-b898-52c93c616644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165008} {:answer/id #uuid "803c89b4-6c2f-560a-ab7f-d10fcdcbe725" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{56}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165009} {:answer/id #uuid "8485d6c1-224e-5b79-97d8-00fe62569ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165010} {:answer/id #uuid "03329300-9cdf-5fc6-b6b3-b2488a608eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165011} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe38f24-8ff9-5aad-9262-6f5a577d63ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03329300-9cdf-5fc6-b6b3-b2488a608eaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{48}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165011} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20f3061-34d4-55c9-a592-6c8209db6f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186165007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1878da63-d2d0-51f1-b7ff-8fd5d5b3b1a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-353417" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\cdot (x+1),\\,y(-1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with one step to approximate $y(0).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the slope at the initial point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table and add the initial condition data.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x+1) \\\\  & =1(-1+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′} & ={y}_{1}({x}_{1}+1) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={y}_{2}({x}_{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ |  |  |\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx) \\\\ =(-1+1,\\,1+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot 1) \\\\ =(0,\\frac{13}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{′} & ={y}_{3}({x}_{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}\\cdot (0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ |  |\n\n- Finally, we compute $Δy$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{8}+\\frac{13}{8})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\frac{31}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{31}{48} \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{8}$ | $\\frac{31}{48}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\frac{79}{48}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈\\frac{79}{48}.$"}]}
